# Publish a hybrid Rocdown site

Hybrid islands are **experimental**. Static Markdown pages are the stable
Rocdown path. Live pages reuse the application layer; the reserved
`@island` syntax is not implemented.

A hybrid site is two artifacts:

1. **CDN tree** — HTML documents, hashed CSS/images, discovery files, and
   the Cmd/Ctrl-K palette. Pages without handlers do not include
   Datastar. `live` pages include initial island HTML and Datastar.js.
2. **Island service** — `rocdown serve-islands` (colocated handlers) or
   `rocci run` on `[http].service`. The CDN file is a snapshot; the
   service owns durable state.

:figure[caption: "Live pages splice compiled islands into CDN HTML. Commands and streams run on a separate island service, not page GET."] {{
    :img[src: "/assets/diagrams/islands.svg", alt: "A .rocdown file splits Markdown, @page, and article blocks into Rust article HTML with placeholders, and @render, templates, and @component through compile_islands into Roc that roc evaluates. After splice, static and hydrate pages have no Datastar; live pages add Datastar and an island service for commands and live streams. There is no @island syntax.", loading: "lazy", decoding: "async"]
}}

Page kinds (`static`, `hydrate`, `live`) are in the
[language reference](/docs/rocdown/language/). `rocdown build --cdn-only`
errors (`RD2302`) when any published page is `live`, so a CDN-only
publish cannot ship action buttons with no service.

`docs/` in this repository is a `static` catalog. The worked example is
`examples/rocdown/counter`.

## Walk the counter

`examples/rocdown/counter/index.rocdown` puts SQLite helpers in `@roc`,
then calls them from `@post:command`. `@render` bakes `count: 0` into the
CDN HTML. On load, a live stream replaces the snapshot. Increment and
reset are commands (empty SSE to Datastar; 204 to `curl`).

```sh
rocdown inspect artifacts examples/rocdown/counter
rocdown run examples/rocdown/counter --no-window
```

Open `/`, click Increment, and confirm `#counter output` morphs. Expect
`index` live and `about` static. The complete command list lives in
`examples/rocdown/counter/README.md`.

## Same-origin deploy

Leave `[http].service_origin` empty. The browser POSTs relative
`/actions/` URLs; CSP `connect-src` is `'self'`. Reverse-proxy
`/actions/` and `/health` to `serve-islands`; serve the rest from the CDN
tree.

```caddy
example.com {
    handle /actions/* {
        reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8001
    }
    handle /health {
        reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8001
    }
    handle {
        root * /var/www/site
        try_files {path} {path}/index.html
        file_server
    }
}
```

1. `rocdown build` the site.
2. Upload `dist/` to the CDN or object store.
3. Run `rocdown serve-islands` with a persistent `DB_PATH`.
4. Point the reverse proxy at that process for `/actions/` and `/health`.

Set an absolute `[http].service_origin` only when the service is on
another origin, then rebuild so action URLs and CSP match. CORS and
cookies for that layout are not shipped.

Docker musl targets and `rocci-ops site` / `serve hybrid` live in
`docker/README.md` and the
[contributor checklist](/docs/reference/contributor/checklist/).
